In quirks and limited-quirks mode, a line box in Ladybird now takes the
strut of its block container only when the block container holds text
on the line directly, when a forced break directly in it is alone on
the line, or when it’s a list item. An inline box brings its line height
to a line only when it holds text there directly, or has borders or
padding at its start or end.

Blink does the same. In quirks mode, its InlineLayoutStateStack::
OnBeginPlaceItems() gives the line box no strut, and LogicalLineBuilder
calls EnsureTextMetrics() for the box around each piece of text
and each forced break it places. A list item keeps its strut on
every line, by a rule in LogicalLineBuilder::CreateLine(). Gecko’s
nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignFrames() sets zeroEffectiveSpanBox for
the root span, and for any span without borders, padding, or margins,
unless it holds non-collapsed text directly. It also counts a box
with a margin alone, and it gives a list item no strut, so it differs
from Ladybird there. WebKit decides the same things in InlineQuirks::
inlineBoxAffectsLineBox(), but it keeps the strut on a line that ends in
a forced break after other content.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit 26.5
ran the 25 cases of the two tests added here. Ladybird gives what Chrome
gives in every case. Firefox differs on the list item and on the empty
span with a margin, and WebKit on the two lines that end in a forced
break after other content.
